<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Back Pain: Best Points" description="Learn how to use acupressure for back pain relief. Step-by-step guide to BL40, BL60, GB21, and other points for lower back, upper back, and sciatica-like pain." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions/" }, { "label": "Acupressure for Back Pain", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-back-pain/" }]} datePublished="2025-04-01" dateModified="2026-06-01" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Back Pain: Best Points for Lasting Relief</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Acupressure targets the root causes of back pain by releasing muscle tension, improving circulation, and activating the body's natural pain relief mechanisms.</p><section id="bl40" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/bl40-weizhong-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">1. BL40 (Weizhong) - Middle of the Crook</a></h2><p class="mb-3">BL40 is the most important acupressure point for lower back pain. Located in the crease behind the knee, this point has a direct meridian connection to the lower back.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> In the center of the crease behind the knee (popliteal fossa).</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> While sitting with your leg bent, use both thumbs to press into the center of the knee crease. Hold for 1-2 minutes per leg.</p></section><section id="bl60" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/bl60-kunlun-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">2. BL60 (Kunlun) - Kunlun Mountains</a></h2><p class="mb-3">BL60 is effective for lower back pain, especially pain that radiates down the leg (sciatica).</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> In the depression between the outer ankle bone and the Achilles tendon.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly into the hollow behind the outer ankle. Hold for 1-2 minutes per leg.</p></section><section id="gb21" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/gb21-jianjing-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">3. GB21 (Jianjing) - Shoulder Well</a></h2><p class="mb-3">GB21 is the primary point for upper back and shoulder tension. It releases tightness that often contributes to upper back pain.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> At the midpoint of the shoulder, directly above the nipple line, in the belly of the trapezius muscle.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Use your opposite hand to press deeply into the shoulder muscle. Hold for 1-2 minutes per side.</p></section><section id="bl23" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/bl23-shenshu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">4. BL23 (Shenshu) - Kidney Shu</a></h2><p class="mb-3">BL23 supports kidney energy, which TCM associates with lower back strength. It is excellent for chronic lower back pain with fatigue.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the lower back, 1.5 finger-widths from the spine at the level of the second lumbar vertebra.</p></section><section id="routine" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Back Pain Relief Routine</h2><ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><li>Start with BL40 (2 minutes each leg) to open the back meridian</li><li>Move to BL60 (1 minute each ankle) for sciatic pain</li><li>Press GB21 (1 minute each shoulder) for upper back tension</li><li>End with BL23 (2 minutes each side) for lower back support</li></ol></section><section id="evidence" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Scientific Evidence</h2><p class="mb-3">Review evidence for acupressure on musculoskeletal pain, including low back pain, is generally positive but limited in quality. Reviews of acupressure for pain (Chen YW &amp; Wang HH, 2014, <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23415783/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 underline decoration-forest-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300 dark:hover:text-forest-200">PMID 23415783</a>) and for symptom relief more broadly (Lee EJ &amp; Frazier SK, 2011, <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21531533/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 underline decoration-forest-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300 dark:hover:text-forest-200">PMID 21531533</a>) describe the evidence as promising while noting that many studies are small or hard to blind. No reliable percentage reduction in back pain is yet established. Treat the routine above as a complement to, not a replacement for, professional back care.</p></section><hr class="my-8"><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> Seek medical evaluation for back pain accompanied by numbness, weakness, bowel/bladder changes, or if pain persists more than 2 weeks.</p></div>
  <EvidenceBox refs={["song-2015", "lee-frazier-2011"]} note={"Evidence for acupressure from published reviews."} />
